    "result_text": "That's a comment-only change. The implementation is complete and verified.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `capacityWarning` flag that returns `true` when the owner's city already holds 40\u201349 owners at creation time (approaching the capacity limit of 50), while leaving the hard 409 rejection at 50 unchanged.\n\nChanges made:\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `capacityWarning` boolean to the owner response schema (next to `bulkSignupWarning`), so the generated `OwnerDto` includes it and MapStruct auto-maps it by name.\n\n2. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 added the `capacityWarning` field (`@Column(name = \"capacity_warning\")`) with getter and setter, mirroring `bulkSignupWarning`.\n\n3. **`rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 added `CAPACITY_WARNING_THRESHOLD = 40` and set the flag in `assignDerivedFields`: `cityOwners >= 40 && cityOwners < 50`. This runs after the existing `rejectWhenCityAtCapacity` guard (which already rejects at \u2265 50), so the flag is computed once and fixed at creation, consistent with the other derived warning flags.\n\n4. **All four `schema.sql` files** (h2, hsqldb, mysql, postgres) \u2014 added the `capacity_warning` column so persistence works across every configured database.\n\nThe reasoning that this is a persisted, creation-time flag (rather than computed on read) follows the established `bulkSignupWarning` pattern, and matches the test: `fillCity(city, 40)` then create \u2192 `capacityWarning == true`.\n\nTest results: the full test suite (`AcceptanceTest`, the only test class with `@Test`) passes \u2014 `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.",
